pq(`ev-p1`, 3, `Hardy-Weinberg`, `The frequency of the allele a is`, `0.6`, [`0.36`, `0.4`, `0.72`], `Recessive plants are aa, so q<sup>2</sup> = 720/2000 = 0.36, and q = 0.6. The value 0.36 is q<sup>2</sup>, and 0.4 is the frequency of A.`);
pq(`ev-p1`, 3, `Hardy-Weinberg`, `The number of heterozygous plants is`, `960`, [`320`, `720`, `1280`], `p = 0.4. 2pq = 2 &times; 0.4 &times; 0.6 = 0.48, and 0.48 &times; 2000 = 960. The number 320 is AA (p<sup>2</sup> = 0.16), and 1280 is all plants with the dominant trait.`, `1280 is the dominant phenotype count, which includes the heterozygotes and AA.`);
pq(`ev-p1`, 4, `Hardy-Weinberg`, `After a few generations of the same conditions, the number of aa plants (population size 2000) will be`, `720`, [`480`, `960`, `1200`], `The population is in equilibrium, so allele and genotype frequencies are constant: 0.36 &times; 2000 = 720.`);

pass(`ev-p2`, `Moths on tree trunks`, `In a woodland, light and dark forms of a moth rest on tree trunks. In 1850 the dark form was 2% of the moths. Soot from factories blackened the trunks and killed the lichens, and by 1950 the dark form was 95%. After clean-air laws in 1970 the lichens returned, and the dark form fell to 20% by 2000. Birds eat the moths they can see.`);
pq(`ev-p2`, 3, `selection`, `The change from 1850 to 1950 is an example of`, `directional selection`, [`stabilising selection`, `disruptive selection`, `genetic drift`], `The population shifted towards one extreme (dark).`);
